// Title: PsExec/PAExec Escalation to LOCAL SYSTEM
// Sigma ID: 8834e2f7-6b4b-4f09-8906-d2276470ee23
// Status: test | Level: high
// Author: Florian Roth (Nextron Systems), Nasreddine Bencherchali (Nextron Systems)
// Date: 2021-11-23 | Modified: 2024-03-05
// ATT&CK: resource-development, T1587.001
// Description: Detects suspicious commandline flags used by PsExec and PAExec to escalate a command line to
//   LOCAL_SYSTEM rights
// False positives:
//   - Admins that use PsExec or PAExec to escalate to the SYSTEM account for maintenance purposes (rare)
//   - Users that debug Microsoft Intune issues using the commands mentioned in the official documentation; see https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/mem/intune/apps/intune-management-extension
// References:
//   - https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sysinternals/downloads/psexec
//   - https://www.poweradmin.com/paexec/
//   - https://www.fireeye.com/blog/threat-research/2020/10/kegtap-and-singlemalt-with-a-ransomware-chaser.html
// Sigma rule: https://github.com/SigmaHQ/sigma/blob/adf5a626c4a776f3f023056f0f398b9523a2f09e/rules/windows/process_creation/proc_creation_win_sysinternals_psexec_paexec_escalate_system.yml
// License: Detection Rule License (DRL) 1.1 - https://github.com/SigmaHQ/Detection-Rule-License
// Converted by sigma2kql (target: microsoft_xdr, pySigma 1.5.1, pySigma-backend-kusto 1.0.1)
// Validation: passed (Kusto.Language 12.4.1, live Defender schema)
// Machine-generated and not tested against real telemetry. Verify before use as a detection.

